\title{Weekly Report, Week 45, 2026}

\p{
For this week, there is good progress on the Hoare weakest preexpectation logic for probabilistic tail bounds.
I have fixed the following languages: 
(1) An expression/command imperative programming language in the form of pGCL, with random Bernoulli assignment by variable probability. 
This is standard formulation in some variants but extends Batz's pGCL, which only supports branching by a fixed numerical probability.
(2) An expression language for random variables.
(3) An assertion language for expressing inequalities
(4) A logical system supporting Hoare judgments and propositional judgments.
}

\p{
The proof rules for this logic is still to be developed.
However, I now have a weakest-preexpectation transformer in the form of a pullback on random variables.
The basic property of this pullback is such that expectation of the pullback (by a command kernel) in this stage equals the expectation of the original random variable in stage after the kernel executes.
Proving that a random variable satisfies some constraints after executing a command is equivalent to proving that the transformed variable satisfies the constraint now.
This transformer comes directly from Batz's work, and is attributed to Kozen.
There is analogous Kozen backwards-forwards semantic duality theorem to be proved.
}

\p{
More importantly, Batz's work is mostly about \strong{expressiveness}.
This implies they try to take a minimal expression language and try to simulate many syntactic constructs, e.g. sequences, sets, products within it.
This reduces proof obligations for expressiveness because there are fewer cases to check.
The disadvantage of this approach for verification is not many constructs are readily available to describe measurable functions, which is inconvenient.
In my expression language I try to provide more constructs.
I observe that the expressiveness theorem is in fact a soundness theorem of a syntactic transformer, if we allow enough constructs (to be proved).
Curiously, this raises a vague question of what's a good language for expressing the measurable functions that makes it enough to describe a class of them for different verification purpose.
}




